1
00:00:00.066 --> 00:00:03.136
AI agents are software applications
powered by LLMs.

2
00:00:03.603 --> 00:00:07.040
In this first lesson,
you'll learn how evaluating LLM-based systems

3
00:00:07.040 --> 00:00:09.275
differs
from traditional software testing.

4
00:00:09.275 --> 00:00:12.912
We'll then examine what agents
mean and discuss what you need to consider

5
00:00:12.912 --> 00:00:15.782
when evaluating agents. Let's dive in.

6
00:00:15.782 --> 00:00:19.252
When you think about evaluation in
general, there are typically two layers.

7
00:00:20.020 --> 00:00:23.123
On the left-hand side,
you have LLM model evaluation.

8
00:00:23.690 --> 00:00:27.427
This focuses on how well a large language
model performs a specific task.

9
00:00:27.994 --> 00:00:31.831
You might have seen benchmarks
like MMLU for questions spanning math,

10
00:00:31.831 --> 00:00:36.536
philosophy, medicine and more,
or human eval for code generation tasks.

11
00:00:37.037 --> 00:00:40.473
Providers often use these benchmarks
to showcase how their foundation

12
00:00:40.473 --> 00:00:42.342
models stack up.

13
00:00:42.342 --> 00:00:43.476
On the right-hand side,

14
00:00:43.476 --> 00:00:46.479
you have an LLM system
or application evaluation.

15
00:00:46.546 --> 00:00:49.549
This zeroes
in on how well your overall application,

16
00:00:49.549 --> 00:00:52.485
of which the LLM is just a part
of, performs.

17
00:00:52.485 --> 00:00:55.688
Here, the data sets being used for
evaluation are created

18
00:00:55.989 --> 00:00:58.925
either
manually, automatically, or synthetically,

19
00:00:58.925 --> 00:01:00.193
using real-world data.

20
00:01:01.227 --> 00:01:01.561
When you

21
00:01:01.561 --> 00:01:05.799
incorporate an LLM into a broader system
or product, you'll want to understand

22
00:01:05.799 --> 00:01:09.569
if the entire system
including the prompts, tools, memory,

23
00:01:09.702 --> 00:01:12.939
and routing components are delivering
the results that you expect.

24
00:01:13.840 --> 00:01:16.042
You may have experience
with traditional software

25
00:01:16.042 --> 00:01:19.045
testing,
where systems are largely deterministic.

26
00:01:19.212 --> 00:01:21.848
You can picture that
like a train on a track.

27
00:01:21.848 --> 00:01:25.051
There's usually a well-defined start
and end, and it's often straightforward

28
00:01:25.051 --> 00:01:28.421
to check whether each piece, the train
or the track is working correctly.

29
00:01:29.022 --> 00:01:30.156
LLM-based systems,

30
00:01:30.156 --> 00:01:33.460
on the other hand, can feel more
like driving a car through a busy city.

31
00:01:33.893 --> 00:01:37.363
The environment is variable
and the system is non-deterministic.

32
00:01:38.331 --> 00:01:39.699
With software testing,

33
00:01:39.699 --> 00:01:42.902
you rely on unit tests
to check individual parts of the system

34
00:01:43.203 --> 00:01:46.339
and integration tests to make sure
they fit together as intended.

35
00:01:46.773 --> 00:01:49.709
And the results
are usually pretty deterministic.

36
00:01:49.709 --> 00:01:52.779
Unlike traditional software testing,
when you give the same prompt

37
00:01:52.779 --> 00:01:56.316
to an LLM multiple times, you might see
slightly different responses.

38
00:01:56.516 --> 00:01:59.519
Just like how drivers
can behave differently in city traffic.

39
00:01:59.519 --> 00:02:02.755
You're often dealing with more qualitative
or open-ended metrics,

40
00:02:02.989 --> 00:02:05.992
like the relevance
or coherence of the output, which might

41
00:02:05.992 --> 00:02:09.028
not fit neatly into a strict pass/fail
test model.

42
00:02:09.929 --> 00:02:13.299
There are a number of common
evaluation types for LLM systems.

43
00:02:13.833 --> 00:02:15.135
We have hallucination.

44
00:02:15.135 --> 00:02:19.072
Is the LLM accurately using provided
context or as in making things up?

45
00:02:19.539 --> 00:02:20.807
Retrieval relevance.

46
00:02:20.807 --> 00:02:23.076
If the system retrieves
documents or context,

47
00:02:23.076 --> 00:02:25.278
are they actually relevant to the query?

48
00:02:25.278 --> 00:02:27.046
Question answering accuracy.

49
00:02:27.046 --> 00:02:29.816
Does the response match the ground truth
the user needs?

50
00:02:29.816 --> 00:02:30.717
Toxicity.

51
00:02:30.717 --> 00:02:33.820
Is the LLM outputting harmful
or undesirable language?

52
00:02:34.053 --> 00:02:35.321
And overall performance.

53
00:02:35.321 --> 00:02:38.191
How well is the system
performing at its goal?

54
00:02:38.191 --> 00:02:40.660
There are many open-source tools
and data sets that can help

55
00:02:40.660 --> 00:02:44.164
you measure these aspects, as well as help
you develop your own evals.

56
00:02:44.464 --> 00:02:46.566
And we'll cover a few
of these later in the course.

57
00:02:47.800 --> 00:02:50.837
As soon as you move from an LLM-based
app to an agent,

58
00:02:50.970 --> 00:02:53.973
you're adding an extra layer
of complexity.

59
00:02:54.073 --> 00:02:58.711
Agents use LLMs for reasoning,
but they also take actions on your behalf

60
00:02:59.045 --> 00:03:02.949
by choosing among tools, APIs,
or other capabilities.

61
00:03:03.616 --> 00:03:06.019
an agent as a software-based system

62
00:03:06.019 --> 00:03:09.656
that can take actions on behalf of a user
utilizing reasoning.

63
00:03:09.956 --> 00:03:12.959
An agent
typically has three main components.

64
00:03:12.959 --> 00:03:16.529
We have reasoning,
which is powered by the LLM. Routing,

65
00:03:16.729 --> 00:03:21.467
which is deciding which tool or skill
to use. And the action, which is executing

66
00:03:21.467 --> 00:03:24.404
the tool call, API call, or code.

67
00:03:24.404 --> 00:03:27.974
You may have already seen
examples of agent use cases, like personal

68
00:03:27.974 --> 00:03:31.811
assistant agents that help you take notes
or transcribe information for you.

69
00:03:32.111 --> 00:03:35.782
Desktop or browser-based agents
that help automate repetitive tasks.

70
00:03:36.216 --> 00:03:39.752
Agents for data scraping and summarization
and agents that can conduct

71
00:03:39.752 --> 00:03:42.088
search and compile research.

72
00:03:42.088 --> 00:03:45.792
Let's use an example use case
to illustrate how an agent actually works.

73
00:03:46.292 --> 00:03:49.295
Suppose you want an agent to book a trip
to San Francisco.

74
00:03:49.495 --> 00:03:51.631
There's a lot going on behind the scenes.

75
00:03:51.631 --> 00:03:55.235
First, the agent has to figure out
which tool or API

76
00:03:55.235 --> 00:03:57.403
it should call based on your request.

77
00:03:57.403 --> 00:03:58.538
It needs to understand

78
00:03:58.538 --> 00:04:01.507
what you're really asking for
and which resources will help.

79
00:04:01.908 --> 00:04:05.378
Next, it might call a search API
to check available flights

80
00:04:05.378 --> 00:04:09.816
or hotels, and it could decide to ask you
follow-up questions or refine

81
00:04:09.816 --> 00:04:12.051
how it constructs the request
for that tool.

82
00:04:12.051 --> 00:04:15.321
Finally, you wanted to return a friendly
and accurate response,

83
00:04:15.588 --> 00:04:17.523
ideally with the correct trip details.

84
00:04:18.558 --> 00:04:21.561
Now, let's think
about how you'd evaluate the step by step.

85
00:04:21.594 --> 00:04:24.130
Did the agent pick the right tool
in the first place?

86
00:04:24.130 --> 00:04:27.567
When it forms a search or booking request
that it call the correct function

87
00:04:27.567 --> 00:04:29.102
with the right parameters?

88
00:04:29.102 --> 00:04:30.503
Is it using your contacts,

89
00:04:30.503 --> 00:04:33.606
for instance, the dates, preferences,
and location accurately?

90
00:04:34.340 --> 00:04:35.908
How does the final response look?

91
00:04:35.908 --> 00:04:38.911
Does it have the right tone
and is it factually correct?

92
00:04:39.679 --> 00:04:42.315
In the system,
there's plenty that can go wrong.

93
00:04:42.315 --> 00:04:45.551
Maybe the agent returns flights
to San Diego instead of San Francisco.

94
00:04:45.952 --> 00:04:47.253
That might work for some people.

95
00:04:47.253 --> 00:04:48.621
Then those folks would be unhappy

96
00:04:48.621 --> 00:04:51.591
if they wanted San Francisco
and ended up with San Diego.

97
00:04:51.791 --> 00:04:55.962
This highlights why you need to evaluate
not just the LLMs raw output,

98
00:04:56.195 --> 00:05:00.500
but also how the agent decides
on each action along the way.

99
00:05:01.234 --> 00:05:05.905
You might see issues like the agent
calling the wrong tool, misusing context,

100
00:05:06.172 --> 00:05:09.175
or even adopting a snarky
or inappropriate tone.

101
00:05:09.609 --> 00:05:12.612
Sometimes users
also try to jailbreak the system,

102
00:05:12.612 --> 00:05:15.615
which can create even more unexpected
outputs.

103
00:05:15.748 --> 00:05:17.617
To evaluate each of these factors.

104
00:05:17.617 --> 00:05:20.620
You can use human feedback
or human in the loop or

105
00:05:20.620 --> 00:05:23.823
in less an LLM itself
as a judge to assess

106
00:05:23.823 --> 00:05:27.427
whether the agent's final response
is truly fulfilling your requirements.

107
00:05:27.727 --> 00:05:31.331
We'll dive deeper into how LLM
as a judge works with agent evaluation

108
00:05:31.331 --> 00:05:32.165
later in this course.

109
00:05:33.366 --> 00:05:36.402
Finally, remember
that even small changes to your prompts

110
00:05:36.402 --> 00:05:39.405
or code can have unexpected
ripple effects.

111
00:05:39.405 --> 00:05:42.942
For instance, adding a simple line
in like "remember to respond

112
00:05:42.942 --> 00:05:46.079
politely" might help you improve
on a number of use cases,

113
00:05:46.412 --> 00:05:49.782
but can also cause a regression
in test cases that you may not expect.

114
00:05:50.416 --> 00:05:54.187
That's why you want to maintain
a representative set of test cases or data

115
00:05:54.187 --> 00:05:58.658
sets that reflect your critical use cases.
Each time you adjust your system,

116
00:05:58.991 --> 00:06:02.929
you can rerun evals on these data
sets to catch regressions

117
00:06:03.162 --> 00:06:06.332
and keep building out
new agent capabilities over time.

118
00:06:06.766 --> 00:06:10.670
This approach is key to developing robust
agent evaluations.

119
00:06:11.871 --> 00:06:13.773
Just like you do
with traditional software.

120
00:06:13.773 --> 00:06:16.342
You want to iterate on an agent's
performance.

121
00:06:16.342 --> 00:06:19.412
However, you can't rely on purely
deterministic checks.

122
00:06:19.879 --> 00:06:23.216
Agents themselves are non-deterministic,
and take multiple paths,

123
00:06:23.383 --> 00:06:26.519
and might regress in one scenario
even as they improve in another.

124
00:06:27.320 --> 00:06:30.323
To handle this,
you want a consistent set of tests

125
00:06:30.490 --> 00:06:34.794
covering different user cases
that get run every time you make a change.

126
00:06:35.628 --> 00:06:38.030
These tests
often loop back from production data,

127
00:06:38.030 --> 00:06:41.100
like real-world
queries and real user interactions.

128
00:06:41.401 --> 00:06:45.872
Back to development, where you can refine
on prompts, tools, or your approach.

129
00:06:46.406 --> 00:06:49.609
This can help you catch regressions
when your agent is deployed in production,

130
00:06:49.942 --> 00:06:53.746
and continuously expand your test coverage
to improve on your system over time.

131
00:06:54.347 --> 00:06:57.483
A few of the tools that we're going
to be covering in this course are: trace

132
00:06:57.483 --> 00:07:00.253
instrumentation to understand
what's happening with your agent

133
00:07:00.253 --> 00:07:04.524
underneath the hood, an eval runner
which contains LLM as a judge,

134
00:07:05.291 --> 00:07:09.095
data sets which you can use to rerun
experiments, human feedback

135
00:07:09.262 --> 00:07:12.265
that allow you to capture
human annotations and production,

136
00:07:12.565 --> 00:07:16.035
and a prompt playground you can use
for your data for iterating on your data.

137
00:07:17.236 --> 00:07:17.570
In this

138
00:07:17.570 --> 00:07:21.541
lesson, you took a broad
look at how LLM model evaluation

139
00:07:21.541 --> 00:07:25.011
compares to system evaluation,
why LLM-based apps

140
00:07:25.011 --> 00:07:27.980
require different testing approaches
than traditional software,

141
00:07:28.281 --> 00:07:32.485
the extra complexity that agents
bring with reasoning, routing, and action.

142
00:07:32.985 --> 00:07:37.256
Common pitfalls with agents like
wrong tool selection for context usage

143
00:07:37.557 --> 00:07:41.394
and the importance of iterative testing
using a single set of tools

144
00:07:41.394 --> 00:07:43.396
for development to production.

145
00:07:43.396 --> 00:07:46.899
You'll get to see all of this in action
and dive into practical code soon.

146
00:07:47.433 --> 00:07:50.236
In the following lessons,
you'll look even more closely

147
00:07:50.236 --> 00:07:54.140
at how to evaluate an agent,
what data to collect, and how to structure

148
00:07:54.140 --> 00:07:57.643
these evaluations to keep your agent
on track in the real world.

